{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "R4ALL_SOP_FairDataAssessment.pdf", "element_type": "CompositeElement", "page": 1, "doc_id": "430a1613-8c9f-470d-bd4b-9b09b21d7240"}, "page_content": "[Source: R4ALL_SOP_FairDataAssessment.pdf]\n\n2 Goal and Area of Application\n\nFAIR data assessment for experimental data is an essential process that ensures data is managed in accordance with the FAIR (Findability, Accessibility, Interoperability, and Reusability) principles. By adhering to these principles, researchers can significantly enhance the transparency, efficiency, and impact of their work beyond the project. Ultimately, a FAIR compliant data resource not only promotes collaboration and innovation but also ensures compliance with institutional and funding agency requirements, ultimately advancing the scientific community's collective knowledge.\n\nThis SOP describes a potential approach to evaluate the FAIRness level of the datasets and proposes opportunities for data dissemination into public repositories.\n\n3 Definitions and Abbreviations\n\nFAIR Findable, Accessible, Interopable, and Reusable FAIR-DSM FAIRplus DataSet Maturity Model\n\nMetadata Data that provides information about other data (Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metadata).\n\n4 Method\n\nGeneral\n\n4.1\n\nAssessment of FAIRness level of dataset\n\nThe principles of FAIR have been applied across multiple domains, including life sciences and drug discovery. Given the complex requirements for FAIR data and its associated metadata, a wide array of tools has been developed to assist, guide, and educate those who are not experts in FAIR principles. Many of these tools are cataloged on the FAIRassist.org platform (https://fairassist.org/).\n\nAmong these tools, we highlight the FAIRplus DataSet Maturity Model (FAIR-DSM) (https://fairplus.github.io/Data-Maturity/). Developed within the IMI project FAIRplus, this tool enables the assessment of the FAIR level of a dataset. It offers an exhaustive list of tasks to help users enhance the FAIRness of their datasets in three key areas: content, representation, and hosting environment (Figure 1). By following these guidelines, users can systematically improve the quality and usability of their data.\n\nR4ALL - SOP \u2013 FAIR data assessment\n\nPage: 2/9\n\nHosted by V <<DESCRIPTIVE & STRUCTURAL METADATA REQUIREMENTS> > HosTING ENVIRONMENT DATASET DESCRIPTOR Hosted by STORAGE CAPABILITY RETRIEVAL CAPABILITY SEARCH CAPABILITY [te me Dataset ane } [ se : : \u2014 the ] Described by How Dataset & How Dataset & Descriptor How Dataset & Descriptor Ps eased tases USSCl PAO Descriptor are stored are retrieved are indexed . 2 DSM-[x]-H1 INDICATORS DSM-[x]-H2 INDICATORS DSM-[X]-H3 INDICATORS c & Conforms to Belongs to << DATA REQUIREMENTS> > << CONTEXTUAL METADATA> > DATASET MODEL DOMAIN MODEL How the Dataset is What's included in the represented Dataset ju 4 [ How the domain model is presented } [ What's reported in the Domain Model } DSM-[X]-C2 INDICATORS rig | 13 (0SM-3-82] Stannard Mona. | DSM-{x]-R1 INDICATORS DSM-[x]-C1 INDICATORS | 14 [DSM-4-R2] Senanmic MoveL J LS 2 [DSM-5-R2} DATA ELEMENT MODEL CONTENT & CONTEXT INDICATORS REPRESENTATION & FORMAT INDICATORS HOSTING CAPABILITIES INDICATORS\n\nFigure 1: FAIR-DSM domain and infrastructure overview. Figure taken from https://fairplus.github.io/Data-Maturity/\n\nConsidering the three aforementioned dimensions (i.e. Content and Context, Representation and Format, and Hosting Capabilities), the FAIR-DSM assigns a maturity level to the dataset. This maturity level is a metric indicating the ease of data shareability, ranging from 1 to 5. Level 1 indicates minimal potential for data reuse, while Level 5 represents an enterprise-level standard for sharing and reusing data across communities (Figure 2). This systematic approach helps researchers understand and improve the FAIRness of their datasets, ultimately promoting more effective data management and collaboration.\n\n5 Managed Data Assets [2 Semantically Typed Data ss _ \u00b0 Community Level. Data at this level complies with community standard domain models, [s] Standardised Data terminologies and formats, and is hosted in an environment offering searching and retrieval capabilities. SS = Project Level. All datasets generated within a project are consistently described Described Data against a locally defined schema, controlled terminologies, and hosted in an environment offering data catalogue level searching capabilities. y ; Data Object level. Data at this level is identifiable as individual generic Identifiable Data data objects and described by generic metadata elements. Hosting environment offers limited retrieval capabilities. a | No potential for re-use beyond lifetime of the research project ow _, Enterprise Level. Data at this level is optimally managed at the most granular level in an environment offering data governance, master data management and reference data management capabilities. Cross-community Level. This level focuses on cross-domain interoperability and is meant to be the level required for larger harmonization and integration projects.\n\nFigure 2: FAIR-DSM maturity levels. Figure taken from https://fairplus.github.io/Data- Maturity/\n\nR4ALL - SOP \u2013 FAIR data assessment\n\nPage: 3/9\n\n4.2\n\nAssessment of FAlIRness level of dataset\n\nThe process of evaluation of FAIR level of a dataset involves the several key steps:\n\n1. Nomination of a dataset\n\n2. Identification of stakeholders\n\nAMRWN\n\n3. Pre-FAIRification assessment\n\n4. Task force establishment\n\n5. Agile-based tasks sprints\n\n6. Post-FAIRification assessment\n[Figure] Diagram maps metadata architecture: a central Dataset (with Dataset Field and Field Value Set) and arrows showing it is \"described by\" Dataset Descriptor, \"conforms to\" Dataset Model, \"belongs to\" Domain Model and is \"hosted by\" Hosting 